<fo:block font-weight="bold" font-style="normal" keep-with-next="always">Programmer</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">
	Brazos County
      
Data Processing Department
    </fo:inline> (Bryan, Texas)</fo:block>
<fo:block font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">April 1983–August 1984</fo:block>
</fo:block>
<fo:block provisional-distance-between-starts="0.5em">
      Responsible for program maintenance and new program development as
      well as system operations and ordering expendable supplies.
      Maintained and enhanced existing office automation and accounting
      systems.
    </fo:block>
<fo:block>
<fo:block font-weight="normal" font-style="italic" keep-with-next="always">Projects:</fo:block>
<fo:list-block provisional-label-separation="1.0em" provisional-distance-between-starts="0.750em" space-after="0.750em">
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Jury Selection
	  Replaced the Jury Selection system that was provided by an
	  outsourced service provider. Provided services to municipal
	  and county courts. Corrected a serious flaw in the outsourced
	  application.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Motor Vehicle Registration
	  Implemented a new Motor Vehicle Registration system.  This
	  system handled all aspects of motor vehicle registration.
	  Interaction with the State of Texas Motor Vehicle Registration
	  System was built into the new system. This system replaced an
	  outsourced application from a service provider, improving
	  service and reducing costs.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Voter Registration
	  Implemented a new Voter Registration system. This system
	  handled all aspects of voter registration, including
	  applications, renewals and compliance with state regulations.
	  Replaced previous system that was an outsourced application
	  from a service provider.  Improved county services and reduced
	  costs.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Office Telephone System
	  Implemented a new telephone system for the county.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
</fo:list-block>
</fo:block>
</fo:block>
<fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always" space-after="0.4em">
<fo:block font-weight="bold" font-style="normal" keep-with-next="always">Student Technician</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">
	Texas A&amp;M University
      
Robotics Automation Laboratory,
Robotics Productivity Group
    </fo:inline> (College Station, Texas)</fo:block>
<fo:block font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">May 1982–May 1983</fo:block>
</fo:block>
<fo:block provisional-distance-between-starts="0.5em">
      Responsible for all computer operations for a research laboratory.
    </fo:block>
<fo:block>
<fo:block font-weight="normal" font-style="italic" keep-with-next="always">Projects:</fo:block>
<fo:list-block provisional-label-separation="1.0em" provisional-distance-between-starts="0.750em" space-after="0.750em">
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Robotics Research
	  Research in methods, technology and technics in support of a
	  grant from the Department of the Air Force, United States of
	  America. This research was in support of <fo:inline font-weight="bold">Just in
	  Time Manufacturing</fo:inline>.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Software Development Support
	  Provided support for graduate students in the Robotics
	  Automation Laboratory. This included hardware support and
	  software development tasks.  Included software for
	  microcontrollers, robots and minicomputers.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Minicomputer Installation
	  Installed and maintained a minicomputer system (Texas
	  Instruments 990/12). Wrote software to interface the
	  minicomputer to various instruments, robots and manufacturing
	  machines.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
</fo:list-block>
</fo:block>
</fo:block>
<fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always" space-after="0.4em">
<fo:block font-weight="bold" font-style="normal" keep-with-next="always">Programmer</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">
	Texas Instruments
      
Corporate Engineering Center
    </fo:inline> (Dallas, Texas)</fo:block>
<fo:block font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">June 1980–June 1982</fo:block>
</fo:block>
<fo:block provisional-distance-between-starts="0.5em">
      Primarily responsible for software development to support the TI
      99/4A microcomputer.  Employed and based in Dallas, Texas, while
      working independently in College Station, Texas.
    </fo:block>
<fo:block>
<fo:block font-weight="normal" font-style="italic" keep-with-next="always">Projects:</fo:block>
<fo:list-block provisional-label-separation="1.0em" provisional-distance-between-starts="0.750em" space-after="0.750em">
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Automated Software Conversion
	  Developed applications that translated software, primarily
	  educational, from the Apple ][ and RadioShack TRS-80 to the TI
	  99/4(A).
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Communications Software
	  Developed a wide range of communcations software, including a
	  Video Text terminal application and a communications driver
	  for a graphics digitizer tablet.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Texas Agricultural Extension Service
	  Implemented a wide variety of software applications to assist
	  agricultural producers with the management of their
	  production. Provided applications on a wide variety of Texas
	  Instruments computing platforms, from the microcomputer based
	  99/4(A) to the minicomputer 990 family.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item>
<fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" start-indent=".5in">
<fo:block>•</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-label>
<fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
<fo:block>Spreadsheet Application
	  Implemented a VisiCalc-like spreadsheet application for the TI
	  99/4(A). This application support all of the normal
	  spreadsheet functions and several additional, advanced
	  features.
	</fo:block>
</fo:list-item-body>
</fo:list-item>
</fo:list-block>
</fo:block>
</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always" border-bottom-width="thin" border-bottom-style="none" space-after="0.750em" space-before="0.750em" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10pt" start-indent="0in">Local- and wide-area network design and implementation</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">LAN and WAN Technologies: </fo:inline>Ethernet (some), Token-Ring, FDDI, T1, DS3, Analog, Frame Relay, ISDN.</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">Routers and Hubs: </fo:inline>Cisco, Proteon, Wellfleet, 3Com, Synoptics, Cabletron, Xyplex.</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">Communications Hardware: </fo:inline>Various multiplexors, CSU/DSUs, modems, terminal servers.</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">Datalink Protocols and Technologies: </fo:inline>TCP/IP, XNS, IPX, AppleTalk, HDLC, transparent bridging, translational bridging, SLIP, PPP.</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">Application and Routing Protocols: </fo:inline>DHCP, DNS, LDAP, NIS, NIS+, NFS, SNMP, SMTP, ICMP, RIP, NNTP, POP3.</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">Management and Analysis Tools: </fo:inline>Network General Sniffer, Lanalyzer, HP Openview, SunNet Manager, several RMON probes.</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always" border-bottom-width="thin" border-bottom-style="none" space-after="0.750em" space-before="0.750em" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10pt" start-indent="0in">UNIX systems, hardware and software</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">Hardware Platforms: </fo:inline>Sun Sparc, HP 9000.</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic">Operating Systems: </fo:inline>Sun/OS, Solaris 2.X, 5.x, 8, 9, HP-UX 9.X,, AIX, Ultrix, Linux, BSD.</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always" border-bottom-width="thin" border-bottom-style="none" space-after="0.750em" space-before="0.750em" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10pt" start-indent="0in">Network Operating Systems</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic"/>Novell Netware 3.x and 4.x, Windows NT, Banyan Vines, Microsoft LanManager, OS/2 Lan Server, other peer-to-peer NOS's.</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always" border-bottom-width="thin" border-bottom-style="none" space-after="0.750em" space-before="0.750em" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10pt" start-indent="0in">IBM PC compatible systems, hardware and software</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
<fo:inline font-weight="normal" font-style="italic"/>Knowledgeable about most hardware platforms and expansion devices, Knowledgeable about most versions of DOS, Windows and OS/2.</fo:block>
<fo:block keep-with-next="always" border-bottom-width="thin" border-bottom-style="none" space-after="0.750em" space-before="0.750em" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10pt" start-indent="0in">Apple Macintosh compatible systems, hardware and software</fo:block>
<fo:block space-after="0.4em">
